  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B007",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks of unprecedented precision"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B010",
    "B021"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B007 remains my preferred achievement. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture converted exquisite atomic control into a reproducible frequency-measurement capability near 10^-18. This is not merely an incremental clock improvement: it makes time and frequency a practical probe of gravitation and other fundamental effects, while providing a durable metrological instrument. Katori's decisive concept and Ye's defining experimental development form a compact and intelligible attribution.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that outstanding optical-clock performance rests on a broad international metrology community. A final citation must carefully separate the original lattice-clock architecture from later advances in spectroscopy, control, comparison, and clock operation, and should not imply that the two laureates alone produced every aspect of the wider optical-clock revolution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson places B002 first, stressing that Cirac and Zoller supplied foundational proposals for quantum computation and simulation with controllable atomic systems, while ranking B007 fourth because of its major metrological consequences.",
        "response": "I agree that B002 has unusually coherent theoretical attribution and has deeply shaped experiments. But proposals should be judged against the extent to which the central physical capability has become a stable, independently reproducible instrument. For B007 that transition is exceptionally clear: the same core architecture now delivers verified performance and new measurements. This gives the lattice clocks a more sharply delimited experimental accomplishment for a prize at present."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson ranks B010 first because aberration correction made electron microscopy a quantitative sub-ångström instrument with immense scientific reach, while noting that priority across theory and engineering needs historical review.",
        "response": "I share the high assessment of B010 and retain it as a leading alternative. Its observation-enabling impact is analogous in spirit to advanced clocks. My qualification is that corrected electron optics combines several correction architectures and a long chain of instrumental innovations, so the boundary of the single prize-worthy advance and the division among Rose, Haider, and Krivanek need especially rigorous scrutiny. B007 presently has the cleaner conceptual and attribution line."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth ranks B051 second and emphasizes that the recollision picture is a mature, experimentally tested foundation of high-harmonic and attosecond science, while also ranking B007 first.",
        "response": "I agree with the priority she assigns to B007. B051 is a powerful and elegant framework, but its eventual formulation must distinguish the model from the experimental antecedents and from the experimental development of attosecond pulses. The lattice-clock achievement joins an identifiable conceptual innovation to a sustained and independently demonstrated measurement capability with fewer such boundaries."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between Katori and Ye's core contributions and those of other optical-clock pioneers requires detailed documentation.",
      "Whether the intended citation should emphasize precision timekeeping, fundamental tests, or the magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture itself remains to be settled."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
